<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#iron-curtain">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iron Curtain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iron Curtain was the political, military and physical boundary that divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the communist states of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> from the West. The phrase was made famous by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> in a speech at Fulton, Missouri, in March 1946. In many places the division became a fortified border of fences, minefields and watchtowers, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> was the best-known part, and it fell with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a>.</p></div>
